Dictation is a toggle, with a level meter
=========================================

The old control streamed text into the draft as Whisper finished each segment, on its own schedule.
To the person holding the microphone that is indistinguishable from a timer: words appear, stop,
appear again, while they are still deciding what to say, and the text they are about to edit moves
under them half a sentence at a time.

Now it is a switch. Press once: the microphone opens, the button says Listening, and a small meter
beside it shows the input level so there is visible evidence of sound in the room — a light that says
"on" whether or not the microphone is in the room is not an indicator. Press again: capture stops,
the engine waits for the last segment to finish rather than cutting the sentence off, and the whole
thing lands in the box at once, once.

Only the text produced during that press is inserted: the transcript at the moment of starting is
kept as a baseline and subtracted, so a previous dictation is not duplicated into the draft.

`Voice.dictationStatus` is the new call behind the meter — transcript, level, whether the service
believes it is hearing speech, and whether it is still transcribing. The engine's `/state` was always
publishing that; nothing was reading it.

